// [COMMON SUPPORT] Base type for a deployed module's service class. Intentionally EMPTY.
//
// It is registered up front so that cross-cutting service logic can be added ONCE, later, without editing modules
// that are already deployed. Have every module's service extend it:
//
//     public class OrdersService extends {{PKG}}.support.BaseService { ... }
//
// Anything added here then reaches every module the next time it is (re)deployed, and no module source changes.
//
// This lives in the HOST app, not in a module, and it has to. A module deploys as SOURCE and protean's
// RuntimeCompiler builds its classpath from java.class.path, inherited from this JVM — a supertype the module
// names must resolve here. That is the same reason the module-facing dependencies sit in build.gradle's
// [module-facing host classpath] block.
//   - isolation.mode=in-process / worker(embed) / container(embed): the host classpath is what the module sees, so
//     this works as written.
//   - worker.runtime=sidecar: the worker runs an EXTERNAL artifact with a consumer-curated shared-api jar. This
//     package must be in that jar, or the module fails to compile at its first deploy.
//
// WHAT BELONGS HERE: helpers every service may call, protected template methods, shared logging or context
// accessors. WHAT DOES NOT: mutable state. The class is shared across module ClassLoaders, so a mutable static
// here leaks between modules and pins them against unload.
//
// @Transactional BELONGS ON THE SERVICE — this layer — and nowhere else. Not on the Mapper (a MyBatis Mapper is a
// proxy from MapperFactoryBean; the annotation there is not honoured, so it reads as a boundary and is not one)
// and not on the Controller (that would wrap request parsing and serialisation in the transaction).
//
// But it does NOT work by default, and it fails SILENTLY: a module lives in a CHILD ApplicationContext, while
// @EnableTransactionManagement and Boot's transaction autoconfiguration ran in the HOST context — and Spring's
// BeanPostProcessors are per-context, so the host's transaction advisor never proxies a module bean. The method
// simply runs with no transaction and no error. Either the module's own @Configuration adds
// @EnableTransactionManagement + a PlatformTransactionManager, or inject the host's PlatformTransactionManager and
// use a TransactionTemplate. If you add shared transaction plumbing for every module, here is where it goes.
//
// Keep it abstract: it is a seam, never a bean on its own.
package {{PKG}}.support;

public abstract class BaseService {
}
